# Checklist layout guard

**Do not edit Check List (CL) layout unless the user explicitly instructs you to.**

This applies even when the task is about workbench, admin full-width, quote objects, scope builder, bundled SKUs, or shared components.

## Forbidden without explicit CL layout instruction

- Changing CL grid columns, row structure, field order, widths, padding, or alignment
- Editing `src/components/cl-checklist-layout.ts`
- Changing CL markup/classes in `project-checklist-panel.tsx` where `mode !== "workbench"`
- Changing CL rendering in `scope-line-bundled-children.tsx` (`mode="checklist"`)
- Refactoring, “cleaning up”, or “improving” CL layout while doing another task
- Copying admin/workbench full-width or table patterns into checklist mode

## Allowed without a CL layout request

- CL **data** logic (save, fetch, scope answers, line patches) if the task requires it — **no className / layout / grid changes**
- Workbench layout changes (`mode === "workbench"`) when the user asks about workbench or says **layout rules** (see `layout-rules.mdc`)
- Bug fixes that are clearly non-visual (API, types, validation) with zero layout diff

## When the user *does* ask for CL layout changes

1. Treat **`src/components/cl-checklist-layout.ts`** as the single source of truth for CL row layout.
2. Change **only** what they specify — row by row, field by field. Do not “fix” adjacent CL layout.
3. Do **not** change workbench table layout in the same pass unless they ask.
4. After edits, say exactly which CL files and sections changed.

## Trigger phrases (CL layout is in scope)

Only then may you edit CL layout:

- “checklist layout”, “CL layout”, “check list layout”
- Explicit row/column instructions on the checklist page (e.g. “in CL reduce Non Std button height”)
- “layout rules checklist” or user points at `cl-checklist-layout.ts`

If unclear, ask before touching any CL layout code.
